1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,162
WWW.MY-SUBS.CO

1
00:00:50,520 --> 00:00:52,710
Oh, Mr Barrow, you're back.

2
00:00:52,760 --> 00:00:56,470
I'm afraid you've missed luncheon,
but you're in time to help upstairs.

3
00:00:56,520 --> 00:00:59,630
~ Maybe there's something left.
~ Don't bother. I'm not hungry.

4
00:00:59,680 --> 00:01:02,630
~ Charming as ever (!)
~ How's your father doing?

5
00:01:02,680 --> 00:01:06,070
Oh, much better, as it happens. In
fact, he's quite well again.

6
00:01:06,120 --> 00:01:08,440
~ That's good news.
~ Yes. It is.

7
00:01:11,040 --> 00:01:13,870
You remember we're meeting Mr Wavell
at three? Pip's Corner.

8
00:01:13,920 --> 00:01:16,990
Yes, but I don't see the point in
it, since I'm not going to agree.

9
00:01:17,040 --> 00:01:19,150
He's driven all the way from Leeds.

10
00:01:19,200 --> 00:01:22,670
I had a letter from your father.
He says he's in London for a visit.

11
00:01:22,720 --> 00:01:25,590
Shrimpie's in England?
Have you made any plans to see him?

12
00:01:25,640 --> 00:01:29,030
Not yet. He wonders if you might let
him stay here.

13
00:01:29,080 --> 00:01:30,830
Of course, whenever he wants.

14
00:01:30,880 --> 00:01:32,950
~ Is Susan with him?
~ No.

15
00:01:33,000 --> 00:01:35,950
Their time's up soon and she
couldn't face another voyage.

16
00:01:36,000 --> 00:01:39,040
Where will they send him, do you
think? After Bombay.

17
00:01:41,560 --> 00:01:43,950
How are your Russians getting on?

18
00:01:44,000 --> 00:01:45,790
Oh, it's so sad.

19
00:01:45,840 --> 00:01:51,070
They talk about the old days -
dances at the Winter Palace, picnics
on the banks of the Neva -

20
00:01:51,120 --> 00:01:54,710
but there are holes in their shoes,
and they've got no money for food.

21
00:01:54,760 --> 00:01:57,190
This is where Tom says it serves them
right.

22
00:01:57,240 --> 00:01:59,950
Well, I don't approve of how things
were managed in Russia,

23
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:03,670
but I'm still sorry for people who
have to make a new life in a foreign
land.

24
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:07,910
Honestly, Papa, every time you
challenge Tom, you sound much more
unreasonable than he is.

25
00:02:07,960 --> 00:02:09,430
Do I?

26
00:02:09,480 --> 00:02:12,800
How's your old beau managing, Mama?
Prince Thingummyjig.

27
00:02:13,320 --> 00:02:15,710
He is not my old beau.

28
00:02:15,760 --> 00:02:18,990
Your father would have called out
any man who said such a thing.

29
00:02:19,040 --> 00:02:22,320
Even so, I do hope you see him
again. Poor old boy.

30
00:02:24,280 --> 00:02:26,280
You don't know anything about it.

31
00:02:28,960 --> 00:02:30,910
How far would the houses stretch?

32
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:32,960
Well, the whole field, really.

33
00:02:34,080 --> 00:02:36,910
But why can't they be built in a
row, like the other streets?

34
00:02:36,960 --> 00:02:41,550
They'd sell more quickly as separate
dwellings, and we'd get a percentage
of the sales.

35
00:02:41,600 --> 00:02:44,710
So, we're paid once, and in return
the field is lost,

36
00:02:44,760 --> 00:02:49,320
the village is spoiled and Mr Wavell
moves on in search of his next
victim.

37
00:02:58,160 --> 00:03:00,630
Why didn't you tell them we were
coming here today?

38
00:03:00,680 --> 00:03:03,430
~ Oh... ~ They'll find out as soon as
Rose gets home.

39
00:03:03,480 --> 00:03:06,710
I didn't want to be a 'topic' for
the rest of luncheon.

40
00:03:06,760 --> 00:03:08,710
But what are we here for?

41
00:03:08,760 --> 00:03:11,630
Well, something's been troubling me.

42
00:03:11,680 --> 00:03:15,590
Oh, why on earth didn't we get a
taxi from the station?

43
00:03:15,640 --> 00:03:17,430
We must be mad.

44
00:03:17,480 --> 00:03:20,150
Well, we'll get a taxi home - that's
for sure.

45
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:22,200
It's saying it's in the crypt.

46
00:03:27,600 --> 00:03:31,510
Oh, Isobel, I didn't think it would
be like this.

47
00:03:31,560 --> 00:03:33,560
No, neither did I.

48
00:03:44,800 --> 00:03:48,630
Aunt Violet? Mrs Crawley?
You never said you were coming.

49
00:03:48,680 --> 00:03:50,510
We wanted to surprise you.

50
00:03:50,560 --> 00:03:53,600
Well, you've succeeded.

51
00:03:54,400 --> 00:03:56,400
Ah, there he is.

52
00:03:58,440 --> 00:04:01,710
I must be the last person you
expected to see.

53
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:03,840
No. I thought you would come.

54
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:08,840
I've brought my cousin, Mrs Crawley.

55
00:04:09,880 --> 00:04:12,150
Do you remember Rostov?

56
00:04:12,200 --> 00:04:13,950
Count Rostov, I think.

57
00:04:14,000 --> 00:04:17,120
Thank you. Yes. Please, ladies.
Please.

58
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:25,830
I'm sorry I didn't say very much...

59
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:27,630
Thank you... at Downton.

60
00:04:27,680 --> 00:04:30,480
But I was... I was so surprised.

61
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,670
You haven't changed. I have.

62
00:04:35,720 --> 00:04:37,310
It's true.

63
00:04:37,360 --> 00:04:41,350
Then, you were the young and
beautiful Countess of Grantham,

64
00:04:41,400 --> 00:04:46,310
turning eyes in a ballroom or out in
your carriage.

65
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:51,680
Now you are the great lady -
imperious, magnificent.

66
00:04:52,560 --> 00:04:55,470
But these are two sides of the same
coin.

67
00:04:55,520 --> 00:04:59,240
Your life has not much altered in
half a century.

68
00:04:59,880 --> 00:05:01,550
It doesn't seem so to me.

69
00:05:01,600 --> 00:05:06,230
Whereas the handsome and powerful
Prince Kuragin,

70
00:05:06,280 --> 00:05:10,440
with his thousands of acres and his
golden palaces...

71
00:05:12,520 --> 00:05:15,320
That man does not exist. Not any
more.

72
00:05:17,280 --> 00:05:21,150
Ah, Mr Molesley. I've put out some
silver to be cleaned.

73
00:05:21,200 --> 00:05:23,190
I'd be grateful if you could see to
it.

74
00:05:23,240 --> 00:05:26,230
~ Very good, Mr Carson.
~ I was looking for Mr Barrow.

75
00:05:26,280 --> 00:05:27,950
~ He's gone out.
~ Precisely.

76
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:30,990
But then I thought: No.
This is down to Mr Molesley.

77
00:05:31,040 --> 00:05:33,160
After all, he is the First Footman.

78
00:05:35,840 --> 00:05:37,840
Oh...

79
00:05:44,240 --> 00:05:48,830
I have thought a good deal about
what you said the other day,

80
00:05:48,880 --> 00:05:51,510
when I asked after the Princess.

81
00:05:51,560 --> 00:05:54,390
That I don't know how or where she
is?

82
00:05:54,440 --> 00:05:56,440
It's true.

83
00:05:58,160 --> 00:06:00,710
We were arrested together,

84
00:06:00,760 --> 00:06:06,590
and when I came out of prison, I
heard she'd been exiled a year
earlier.

85
00:06:06,640 --> 00:06:09,830
~ There must be someone in authority
who - ~ Who?

86
00:06:09,880 --> 00:06:12,350
The Soviet Ambassador?

87
00:06:12,400 --> 00:06:17,350
Do you think he cares where Princess
Irina Kuragin is presently lodged?

88
00:06:17,400 --> 00:06:19,550
Well, what about the Foreign Office?

89
00:06:19,600 --> 00:06:22,950
Do they help the losing side in any
revolution? No.

90
00:06:23,000 --> 00:06:24,790
You mustn't give up hope.

91
00:06:24,840 --> 00:06:27,590
When you go through a storm like
ours,

92
00:06:27,640 --> 00:06:31,150
you give up hope quite early on in
the proceedings.

93
00:06:31,200 --> 00:06:38,150
I agree. Hope is a tease, designed
to prevent us accepting reality.

94
00:06:38,200 --> 00:06:40,390
Oh, you only say that to sound
clever.

95
00:06:40,440 --> 00:06:42,430
I know.

96
00:06:42,480 --> 00:06:44,480
You should try it.

97
00:06:50,600 --> 00:06:54,120
I want to call on the blacksmith.
I'll see you back at the house.

98
00:06:58,320 --> 00:07:00,310
Mrs Patmore?

99
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:03,240
Oh, good afternoon, m'lord. M'lady.

100
00:07:05,480 --> 00:07:07,480
I wish I knew what that was about.

101
00:07:07,960 --> 00:07:11,350
And why the funny look when I asked
after Shrimpie at luncheon?

102
00:07:11,400 --> 00:07:15,070
I suspect he's come to tell Rose
they're getting a divorce.

103
00:07:15,120 --> 00:07:17,990
~ Are you serious?
~ I'm afraid so.

104
00:07:18,040 --> 00:07:20,110
He's hinted at it in his letters,
apparently,

105
00:07:20,160 --> 00:07:22,270
and I gather things have not been
improving.

106
00:07:22,320 --> 00:07:23,950
That's the end of his career.

107
00:07:24,000 --> 00:07:26,390
Well, the Marlboroughs have survived
divorce.

108
00:07:26,440 --> 00:07:30,510
Well, the Marlboroughs are very
rich. Shrimpie hasn't got a bean.

109
00:07:30,560 --> 00:07:32,830
People will drop them like hot
potatoes.

110
00:07:32,880 --> 00:07:36,550
Will we drop Shrimpie?
Susan's your cousin, not him.

111
00:07:36,600 --> 00:07:39,120
No, I won't take sides, whatever
Susan wants.

112
00:07:48,240 --> 00:07:50,240
Would you give me a hand with these?

113
00:07:52,440 --> 00:07:55,550
~ When are you leaving? ~ Lady Mary
wants to get the eight o'clock.

114
00:07:55,600 --> 00:07:59,110
She's going to a dress show with
Lady Rosamund in the afternoon.

115
00:07:59,160 --> 00:08:01,320
~ I'll miss you.
~ So I should hope.

116
00:08:03,600 --> 00:08:06,830
Are you all right? I hope you
haven't let the sergeant worry you.

117
00:08:06,880 --> 00:08:08,880
No, he doesn't worry me.

118
00:08:10,440 --> 00:08:13,040
I just wish we could all forget
about Mr Green.

119
00:08:18,080 --> 00:08:20,760
Why did you want me to go with you
today?

120
00:08:22,400 --> 00:08:26,480
Oh, it was just in case things
turned mawkish.

121
00:08:27,240 --> 00:08:30,910
I prevent Lord Merton saying
anything to embarrass you -

122
00:08:30,960 --> 00:08:33,110
I wanted the same protection myself.

123
00:08:33,160 --> 00:08:38,150
I presume you and Prince Kuragin
were once... attracted to each other.

124
00:08:38,200 --> 00:08:41,480
'Attracted to each other'?
Is that what you call it?

125
00:08:42,840 --> 00:08:47,710
As it happens, he asked me to run
away with him.

126
00:08:47,760 --> 00:08:52,550
~ But you didn't go. ~ No, Lord Grantham
gave me a frame by Faberge,

127
00:08:52,600 --> 00:08:57,150
with two pictures of the children in
it, and I saw sense.

128
00:08:57,200 --> 00:09:00,030
Lord Grantham sounds rather more
subtle than I'd realised.

129
00:09:00,080 --> 00:09:02,510
Well, like all Englishmen of his
type,

130
00:09:02,560 --> 00:09:07,030
he hid his qualities beneath a thick
blanket of convention,

131
00:09:07,080 --> 00:09:10,830
so I didn't see who he really was at
first.

132
00:09:10,880 --> 00:09:12,880
It was lucky you found out in time.

133
00:09:13,640 --> 00:09:15,590
If it was in time.

134
00:09:15,640 --> 00:09:18,160
I forget.

135
00:09:19,320 --> 00:09:23,390
Daisy! Will you put those bally
books away and come and help?

136
00:09:23,440 --> 00:09:25,150
And where are the pastry casings?

137
00:09:25,200 --> 00:09:27,710
~ I'll make them now.
~ You should have made them before!

138
00:09:27,760 --> 00:09:31,230
~ What is all that? ~ I'm studying the
Glorious Revolution of 1688.

139
00:09:31,280 --> 00:09:34,950
Well, there'll be a glorious
revolution down here, if you don't
watch it!

140
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:36,750
Did you hear that in there?

141
00:09:36,800 --> 00:09:41,000
I mean, mathematics is one thing.
She's studying to be a revolutionary
now!

142
00:09:41,440 --> 00:09:44,910
I hope there's no cooking going on
up in your room.

143
00:09:44,960 --> 00:09:48,560
~ I should find out what he's up to.
~ I'm sure it's nothing.

144
00:10:11,280 --> 00:10:12,830
Lord Merton?

145
00:10:12,880 --> 00:10:16,080
Oh! I er... Well, that is...

146
00:10:17,880 --> 00:10:19,670
You're here.

147
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:22,200
~ Well, hadn't you better come in?
~ Thank you.

148
00:10:26,400 --> 00:10:28,400
Well, how can I help?

149
00:10:36,920 --> 00:10:39,110
I nearly flunked it,

150
00:10:39,160 --> 00:10:41,590
but I've put it off so many times,

151
00:10:41,640 --> 00:10:44,990
and as I was sitting all alone at
luncheon today, I just thought:

152
00:10:45,040 --> 00:10:47,830
'No. I'm jolly well going over there
to say it.'

153
00:10:47,880 --> 00:10:50,430
I do hope this won't be something
you'll regret.

154
00:10:50,480 --> 00:10:52,520
You may regret it. I won't.

155
00:10:57,360 --> 00:10:59,480
Very well. I'm listening.

156
00:11:01,120 --> 00:11:04,870
I really should go down on one knee,
but I fear I'd never get up again.

157
00:11:07,440 --> 00:11:11,680
I'm sure you realise by now - I'm
asking for your hand in marriage.

158
00:11:13,120 --> 00:11:14,750
Dear Lord Merton, you see -

159
00:11:14,800 --> 00:11:16,920
Please. Please.

160
00:11:19,920 --> 00:11:22,350
I want to be quite clear.

161
00:11:22,400 --> 00:11:26,030
I'm not speaking out of loneliness
or with a view to my comfort.

162
00:11:26,080 --> 00:11:29,320
~ I'm sure -
~ No, you're not.

163
00:11:30,920 --> 00:11:34,710
When men of my age marry, that's
usually the reason.

164
00:11:34,760 --> 00:11:37,310
But my proposal is a romantic one.

165
00:11:37,360 --> 00:11:41,280
I state freely and proudly, Isobel,
that I have fallen in love with you.

166
00:11:42,120 --> 00:11:45,320
And I want to spend what remains of
my life in your company.

167
00:11:46,000 --> 00:11:48,510
I believe I could make you happy.

168
00:11:48,560 --> 00:11:52,760
At any rate, I... I should very much
like the chance to try.

169
00:11:54,960 --> 00:11:56,960
Well, goodness, Lord Merton...

170
00:11:58,680 --> 00:12:01,400
I freely admit you have taken me by
surprise.

171
00:12:03,480 --> 00:12:07,880
Not with your proposal, but with
your... talk of love.

172
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
I mean it.

173
00:12:12,040 --> 00:12:14,040
And before you refuse me...

174
00:12:15,120 --> 00:12:18,670
.. I'd like to ask you to leave it on
the table, so to speak.

175
00:12:18,720 --> 00:12:20,670
Say nothing for the time being.

176
00:12:20,720 --> 00:12:23,150
Just think about it.

177
00:12:23,200 --> 00:12:26,150
I'm not sure it would change my
answer,

178
00:12:26,200 --> 00:12:28,710
but after such eloquence,

179
00:12:28,760 --> 00:12:32,040
to refuse your request would seem
ungenerous.

180
00:12:34,200 --> 00:12:36,200
I will think about it.

181
00:12:38,520 --> 00:12:40,390
Well...

182
00:12:40,440 --> 00:12:42,440
Now I think I'll take my leave.

183
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:47,790
I suspect ordinary conversation
would be difficult after that.

184
00:12:57,900 --> 00:13:00,850
What do you mean, that man Bricker
is coming back? Why?

185
00:13:00,900 --> 00:13:03,850
'That man Bricker'? What has he done
to deserve such a welcome?

186
00:13:03,900 --> 00:13:05,570
Hasn't he seen what he came to see?

187
00:13:05,620 --> 00:13:10,210
He wants to investigate a possible
link between our picture and a later
work by della Francesca.

188
00:13:10,260 --> 00:13:11,970
Shrimpie is coming this week.

189
00:13:12,020 --> 00:13:16,420
Well, then, he and Mr Bricker will
get to know each other, won't they?

190
00:13:17,300 --> 00:13:19,570
Edith, darling, why so glum?

191
00:13:19,620 --> 00:13:21,690
I'm not glum, am I?

192
00:13:21,740 --> 00:13:23,740
How's your prodigy child?

193
00:13:24,700 --> 00:13:26,610
I haven't seen her for a while.

194
00:13:26,660 --> 00:13:30,010
I was getting rather under their
feet, so I thought I'd give them a
rest.

195
00:13:30,060 --> 00:13:32,370
I knew that would happen.

196
00:13:32,420 --> 00:13:34,610
Why don't you leave it for a few
months?

197
00:13:34,660 --> 00:13:36,410
~ A few months?
~ I should.

198
00:13:36,460 --> 00:13:39,250
Then they'll be pleased to see you
when you do go back.

199
00:13:39,300 --> 00:13:40,930
Any thoughts on Pip's Corner?

200
00:13:40,980 --> 00:13:43,290
I'd love to understand the merits of
the argument.

201
00:13:43,340 --> 00:13:44,970
You want us to do it, don't you?

202
00:13:45,020 --> 00:13:47,850
Well, it's capital with no outlay.
We have to consider it.

203
00:13:47,900 --> 00:13:50,370
Consider it, yes. But it's complex.

204
00:13:50,420 --> 00:13:54,290
Do we really want to start breaking
bits off the estate and selling them
piecemeal?

205
00:13:54,340 --> 00:13:56,810
You wanted to sell land when Matthew
died.

206
00:13:56,860 --> 00:14:00,330
And I was wrong. I didn't see how
many owners would chip away at their
land

207
00:14:00,380 --> 00:14:02,290
until there was nothing viable left.

208
00:14:02,340 --> 00:14:04,130
Anyway, we can talk about it
tomorrow.

209
00:14:04,180 --> 00:14:06,690
Not me.
I'm going up to London first thing.

210
00:14:06,740 --> 00:14:08,970
Aunt Rosamund's taking me to a dress
show.

211
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:12,100
It's good to know you've got your
priorities straight.

212
00:14:21,140 --> 00:14:23,140
Hello? Who's in there?

213
00:14:24,780 --> 00:14:27,330
Please, let me know who's in there.

214
00:14:27,380 --> 00:14:29,330
Go away.

215
00:14:29,380 --> 00:14:31,250
Mr Barrow?

216
00:14:31,300 --> 00:14:33,980
Would you like me to fetch a man to
help you?

217
00:14:36,660 --> 00:14:38,660
Mr Barrow...

218
00:14:39,140 --> 00:14:41,250
What man?

219
00:14:41,300 --> 00:14:44,180
There isn't a man in
this house who'd help me.

220
00:14:45,620 --> 00:14:47,620
What's the matter with you?

221
00:14:49,020 --> 00:14:51,020
Just mind your own business!

222
00:14:58,060 --> 00:15:00,410
Now, you're sure there's no big
evening event?

223
00:15:00,460 --> 00:15:02,770
No, and we're only there one night.

224
00:15:02,820 --> 00:15:05,690
~ When are you seeing His Lordship?
~ Friday morning.

225
00:15:05,740 --> 00:15:07,740
Before we catch the train home.

226
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:10,770
It's right to do it face to face.

227
00:15:10,820 --> 00:15:12,820
But of course I'm dreading it.

228
00:15:23,460 --> 00:15:26,890
~ Daddy!
~ Hello, Sybbie, my love. Kiss.

229
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:31,650
Thank you, Nanny. Morning, George.

230
00:15:31,700 --> 00:15:33,130
Morning, Donk.

231
00:15:33,180 --> 00:15:35,180
Morning, Sybbie. Morning, George.

232
00:15:35,980 --> 00:15:39,330
Do we think Lady Mary made the
train? She was cutting it fine.

233
00:15:39,380 --> 00:15:41,850
Mr Stark saw them both on board.

234
00:15:41,900 --> 00:15:43,900
Carson, may I ask you something?

235
00:15:44,900 --> 00:15:46,890
Is Mrs Patmore quite happy?

236
00:15:46,940 --> 00:15:51,090
I'm afraid there is a particular
matter that has upset her, m'lord.

237
00:15:51,140 --> 00:15:53,140
May I know what it is?

238
00:15:53,780 --> 00:15:56,050
But why make him wait?

239
00:15:56,100 --> 00:15:58,210
You mean I must refuse him now?

240
00:15:58,260 --> 00:16:01,890
Well, you said yourself the whole
idea was ridiculous.

241
00:16:01,940 --> 00:16:07,340
Yes, but I can only tell you, when
it came to it, he was not...
ridiculous.

242
00:16:08,060 --> 00:16:10,050
You surprise me.

243
00:16:10,100 --> 00:16:13,330
One kind word and your judgment
takes flight.

244
00:16:13,380 --> 00:16:17,180
Surely Prince Kuragin has reminded
you what one kind word can do.

245
00:16:19,420 --> 00:16:21,420
He's asked me to think about it.

246
00:16:22,740 --> 00:16:24,740
And I will think about it.

247
00:16:27,940 --> 00:16:31,900
It was Michael's office on the
telephone - there's been a
development.

248
00:16:33,180 --> 00:16:36,100
Apparently, there's a trial going on
in Munich.

249
00:16:36,740 --> 00:16:39,650
Of the leader of a group of thugs
there.

250
00:16:39,700 --> 00:16:43,650
I've read about this. They wear
brown shirts and go around bullying
people.

251
00:16:43,700 --> 00:16:45,570
The leader tried to start a
revolution.

252
00:16:45,620 --> 00:16:47,730
That's it. It was absurd.

253
00:16:47,780 --> 00:16:51,650
Maybe. But I'm afraid we're going to
see a lot more of this sort of thing.

254
00:16:51,700 --> 00:16:54,770
We pushed Germany too hard with our
demands after the war.

255
00:16:54,820 --> 00:16:59,250
It seems it was this gang that got
into a fight with Michael.

256
00:16:59,300 --> 00:17:02,770
I can easily believe it. They're a
horrid bunch, from the sound of it.

257
00:17:02,820 --> 00:17:06,220
Yes, but we might be close to
finding out what happened to him.

258
00:17:06,860 --> 00:17:08,860
That's why his office telephoned?

259
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:17,050
It will be very hard for you, my
darling,

260
00:17:17,100 --> 00:17:21,610
but I promise it's better to know the
truth

261
00:17:21,660 --> 00:17:24,570
than to live in a cloud of mystery
and despair.

262
00:17:24,620 --> 00:17:29,500
But as long as I don't know for
sure, then, in a way, I'm keeping
him alive.

263
00:17:30,700 --> 00:17:32,700
I hate to let go of that.

264
00:17:35,100 --> 00:17:37,410
Can you keep this to yourself, Papa?

265
00:17:37,460 --> 00:17:39,460
Until we're told for certain.

266
00:17:40,500 --> 00:17:44,130
I don't want to put up with Mary's
pity any longer than I have to.

267
00:17:44,180 --> 00:17:48,220
She will pity you, and God knows, so
do I.

268
00:17:53,540 --> 00:17:55,540
I'm fearfully late.

269
00:17:56,540 --> 00:17:58,610
~ Where are my gloves?
~ Don't worry.

270
00:17:58,660 --> 00:18:01,010
It will all be unpacked before you
get back.

271
00:18:01,060 --> 00:18:03,340
You're a dream. I must run.

272
00:18:03,820 --> 00:18:07,370
Oh, wait a minute.
Could you post this?

273
00:18:07,420 --> 00:18:10,650
Or give it to Mead if he hasn't
emptied the box in the hall.

274
00:18:10,700 --> 00:18:15,010
I never said where to meet me, and
Kensington Gardens would be nice and
neutral.

275
00:18:15,060 --> 00:18:16,970
I thought about dining with him
tonight,

276
00:18:17,020 --> 00:18:19,690
but midday seemed more appropriate
for bad news.

277
00:18:19,740 --> 00:18:21,740
Very good, m'lady.

278
00:18:40,660 --> 00:18:43,290
And how is Edith getting on?

279
00:18:43,340 --> 00:18:46,890
~ Actually, she's rather gloomy at the
moment. ~ Do we know why?

280
00:18:46,940 --> 00:18:50,130
She's taken an interest in a child
of one of the tenants,

281
00:18:50,180 --> 00:18:52,330
and now the parents are sick of her.

282
00:18:52,380 --> 00:18:54,810
And why this child in particular?

283
00:18:54,860 --> 00:18:56,970
I don't know.
She took a fancy to it.

284
00:18:57,020 --> 00:18:59,570
Now she seems to have overplayed her
hand.

285
00:18:59,620 --> 00:19:01,580
Golly. That'd be useful.

286
00:19:02,380 --> 00:19:05,290
So, tell me more about this farmer
and his little girl.

287
00:19:05,340 --> 00:19:08,220
~ How did you know it was a girl?
~ Well, you must have said.

288
00:19:08,580 --> 00:19:10,850
He looks after the pigs.

289
00:19:10,900 --> 00:19:12,900
Oh, yummy.

290
00:19:14,900 --> 00:19:17,370
Good heavens. There's Charles Blake.

291
00:19:17,420 --> 00:19:19,420
What's he doing here?

292
00:19:56,020 --> 00:19:59,530
Excuse me. Is this right for Lord
Gillingham?

293
00:19:59,580 --> 00:20:01,850
That doorway. First floor.

294
00:20:01,900 --> 00:20:03,900
Thank you.

295
00:20:25,700 --> 00:20:27,700
~ Hello.
~ Pleased to meet you.

296
00:20:28,100 --> 00:20:30,690
Nice to see you.
Are you shopping for your trousseau?

297
00:20:30,740 --> 00:20:32,010
Ha-ha.

298
00:20:32,060 --> 00:20:35,420
Oh, I think you know Lady Mary
Crawley. Miss Lane Fox.

299
00:20:35,700 --> 00:20:37,250
Oops.

300
00:20:37,300 --> 00:20:39,490
I've suddenly remembered the
significance.

301
00:20:39,540 --> 00:20:41,170
Try not to be an ass, Charles.

302
00:20:41,220 --> 00:20:43,130
Well, we don't know each other,
exactly.

303
00:20:43,180 --> 00:20:45,940
But we certainly know OF each other.
Don't we?

304
00:20:46,380 --> 00:20:49,290
~ It's not often you meet the woman
you were jilted for. ~ Well, I -

305
00:20:49,340 --> 00:20:52,570
Don't worry. I wouldn't want a man
who preferred someone else.

306
00:20:52,620 --> 00:20:55,410
~ You're quite over him, then?
~ Absolutely.

307
00:20:55,460 --> 00:20:57,690
Now I must go.
I'm meeting Ralph Kerr later.

308
00:20:57,740 --> 00:20:59,860
He's very tetchy if he's kept
waiting.

309
00:21:02,700 --> 00:21:05,010
~ I thought you knew each other.
~ No.

310
00:21:05,060 --> 00:21:07,770
And I must say, she wasn't at all
what I imagined.

311
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:09,370
What was that?

312
00:21:09,420 --> 00:21:12,610
I don't know.
Someone meeker and more virtuous.

313
00:21:12,660 --> 00:21:15,660
~ I don't think Mabel's very meek.
~ No. Obviously not.

314
00:21:16,780 --> 00:21:19,810
~ How long are you in London?
~ I go home tomorrow.

315
00:21:19,860 --> 00:21:22,650
I'll see Tony in the morning and
then catch the train.

316
00:21:22,700 --> 00:21:24,690
~ Why? What are you doing tonight?
~ Nothing.

317
00:21:24,740 --> 00:21:27,290
~ Then why not have dinner with me?
~ Don't you have plans?

318
00:21:27,340 --> 00:21:30,170
None that I want to honour.
I'll come for you at eight.

319
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:33,500
Nowhere too swanky.
I haven't brought the right clothes.

320
00:22:05,140 --> 00:22:08,810
~ I'll do them! ~ Mrs Patmore, His
Lordship is asking for you.

321
00:22:08,860 --> 00:22:10,610
Come up to the library, please.

322
00:22:10,660 --> 00:22:13,770
Oh, my heaven! What, now? Oh, Lord!

323
00:22:13,820 --> 00:22:15,850
You look fine. Give me your apron.

324
00:22:15,900 --> 00:22:20,090
Ah, Mr Molesley. Just the man.
Can you cast your eye over the
dining table?

325
00:22:20,140 --> 00:22:23,130
I won't have time before dinner, and
you're the First Footman.

326
00:22:23,180 --> 00:22:25,170
~ I was just going to -
~ While you're up there,

327
00:22:25,220 --> 00:22:27,770
check the bedrooms for Lord
Flintshire and Mr Bricker.

328
00:22:27,820 --> 00:22:31,410
I haven't got a moment and, as Mr
Carson says, you are the First
Footman.

329
00:22:31,460 --> 00:22:33,460
Right. I'm ready.

330
00:22:36,340 --> 00:22:40,890
~ Go on, then, Mr Molesley.
~ It doesn't seem quite fair.

331
00:22:40,940 --> 00:22:43,530
You wanted to be First Footman.
What did you think?

332
00:22:43,580 --> 00:22:45,900
That you'd spend all day with your
feet up?

333
00:22:53,220 --> 00:22:56,060
I thought I'd pop in and see how
Marigold's getting on.

334
00:22:56,940 --> 00:22:59,860
I'm afraid I'm just taking her up
for a rest, m'lady.

335
00:23:00,580 --> 00:23:03,250
They've been playing outside and
she's tired.

336
00:23:03,300 --> 00:23:06,380
~ Does she have to go up right away?
~ I think so, yes, m'lady.

337
00:23:15,020 --> 00:23:17,020
M'lady?

338
00:23:21,140 --> 00:23:26,100
The... the trouble is, Margie thinks
you're unsettling the child.

339
00:23:33,140 --> 00:23:36,650
There are laws that govern the whole
business of war memorials.

340
00:23:36,700 --> 00:23:38,890
So Mr Carson's told me, m'lord.

341
00:23:38,940 --> 00:23:41,930
I appreciate how distressing this is
for you and your family.

342
00:23:41,980 --> 00:23:43,810
Do you, m'lord?

343
00:23:43,860 --> 00:23:46,090
Because I'm not sure Mr Carson does.

344
00:23:46,140 --> 00:23:49,220
Carson is as sorry about it as I am.
Aren't you, Carson?

345
00:23:49,740 --> 00:23:53,410
I'm sorry that Mrs Patmore should be
distressed, m'lord.

346
00:23:53,460 --> 00:23:57,050
But you're not sorry Archie's name's
to be left off the memorial.

347
00:23:57,100 --> 00:24:00,810
He volunteered.
He didn't wait to be called up.

348
00:24:00,860 --> 00:24:06,180
He went to the Front to fight, and
as for his so-called cowardice...

349
00:24:07,100 --> 00:24:10,490
that were like a wound in battle, a
wound to his brain,

350
00:24:10,540 --> 00:24:13,370
so he didn't know what he was doing.

351
00:24:13,420 --> 00:24:15,250
He were a good and decent lad.

352
00:24:15,300 --> 00:24:18,930
~ Of course he was. ~ But doesn't that
make him a casualty of war, m'lord?

353
00:24:18,980 --> 00:24:20,980
Same as our William or any of them.

354
00:24:21,780 --> 00:24:25,610
He could have stayed here, safe and
well, until they came for him.

355
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:29,530
But instead, he chose to
fight for his country.

356
00:24:29,580 --> 00:24:33,290
It may surprise you to learn, Mrs
Patmore, but I agree with you,

357
00:24:33,340 --> 00:24:35,530
even though I can do nothing about
it.

358
00:24:35,580 --> 00:24:37,850
It's not helpful, but I do.

359
00:24:37,900 --> 00:24:39,690
No, it is helpful.

360
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:43,860
It helps to think that decent folk
can see our Archie was a victim.

361
00:24:44,300 --> 00:24:46,460
Even if Mr Carson can't.

362
00:24:49,220 --> 00:24:51,220
Thank you, Carson.

363
00:24:53,100 --> 00:24:56,380
When does Shrimpie get here?
And your ghastly art dealer.

364
00:24:57,060 --> 00:25:00,970
They're on the same train and the
car's meeting them, so they'll have
time to change.

365
00:25:01,020 --> 00:25:03,090
And he isn't a dealer. He's a
historian.

366
00:25:03,140 --> 00:25:05,500
Is he, indeed? Lawks a-mercy.

367
00:25:06,540 --> 00:25:09,010
I wish I knew why he was coming.

368
00:25:09,060 --> 00:25:11,570
~ He wants to discuss the painting.
~ With you?

369
00:25:11,620 --> 00:25:13,620
Yes. Is that so shocking?

370
00:25:14,660 --> 00:25:16,660
I'd better get on.

371
00:25:22,060 --> 00:25:24,250
Oh, Mr Molesley. I've not been told

372
00:25:24,300 --> 00:25:27,610
whether Lord Flintshire and Mr
Bricker will be bringing valets.

373
00:25:27,660 --> 00:25:30,170
Could you make it your business
to see they're properly attended?

374
00:25:30,220 --> 00:25:32,410
Well, I've got a lot on my plate, Mr
Carson.

375
00:25:32,460 --> 00:25:34,770
I am sorry, Mr Molesley,
but you are -

376
00:25:34,820 --> 00:25:37,060
Yes, I know. I know. I'm the First
Footman.

377
00:25:41,140 --> 00:25:43,140
So, you're happy?

378
00:25:43,900 --> 00:25:48,050
Well, I'm not delirious, but who is?
I have a pretty good life.

379
00:25:48,100 --> 00:25:50,100
Well, I'm glad.

380
00:25:52,220 --> 00:25:55,490
I was never the type to die of a
broken heart, you know.

381
00:25:55,540 --> 00:25:57,850
I'm sorry if that offends you.

382
00:25:57,900 --> 00:26:01,740
On the contrary. I only hope Tony
feels the same.

383
00:26:03,540 --> 00:26:05,860
Goodness. I wasn't expecting that.

384
00:26:11,380 --> 00:26:13,530
I wish I could work you out.

385
00:26:13,580 --> 00:26:15,620
I wish I could work me out.

386
00:26:18,660 --> 00:26:20,660
~ When will you tell him?
~ Tomorrow.

387
00:26:21,420 --> 00:26:23,090
I'm meeting him at noon.

388
00:26:23,140 --> 00:26:26,170
By the Peter Pan statue in
Kensington Gardens.

389
00:26:26,220 --> 00:26:28,330
Won't that make him dream of happy
families?

390
00:26:28,380 --> 00:26:30,380
Well, it's too late to change it
now.

391
00:26:31,060 --> 00:26:32,770
What's he done wrong?

392
00:26:32,820 --> 00:26:34,650
Nothing.

393
00:26:34,700 --> 00:26:37,620
I'm very fond of him.
I'll always be fond of him.

394
00:26:39,620 --> 00:26:43,700
I want him to be the godfather of my
children. Just not their father.

395
00:26:45,580 --> 00:26:48,060
~ How will he take it?
~ Not as well as you.

396
00:26:52,060 --> 00:26:56,090
Well, you've got a way to soften the
blow. If you want to.

397
00:26:56,140 --> 00:26:58,380
~ I don't see how.
~ Don't you?

398
00:27:01,980 --> 00:27:05,890
~ You seem a little subdued tonight.
~ Do I, Granny?

399
00:27:05,940 --> 00:27:08,410
How strange. I wonder why.

400
00:27:08,460 --> 00:27:13,210
I know it sounds hard, but you must
learn to leave things behind.

401
00:27:13,260 --> 00:27:16,170
I suppose you mean I'm to leave
Michael behind now,

402
00:27:16,220 --> 00:27:19,490
as you've already made it quite
clear I'm to leave the baby behind.

403
00:27:19,540 --> 00:27:23,370
I would never suggest anything that
is not in your interest.

404
00:27:23,420 --> 00:27:26,330
In my interest? Or the family's?

405
00:27:26,380 --> 00:27:28,730
To me, they are the same.

406
00:27:28,780 --> 00:27:30,780
And that is where we differ.

407
00:27:34,020 --> 00:27:36,210
I've been thinking about your
missing person.

408
00:27:36,260 --> 00:27:38,650
Oh, I don't want to be a nuisance,

409
00:27:38,700 --> 00:27:43,490
but aren't there departments devoted
to finding people who are lost?

410
00:27:43,540 --> 00:27:46,690
Alas, not at my command.
And the Russians who were driven out

411
00:27:46,740 --> 00:27:48,810
have spread to the four corners of
the earth.

412
00:27:48,860 --> 00:27:51,180
Who is this woman?
An old friend of yours?

413
00:27:52,020 --> 00:27:55,050
No, she's the wife of an old friend.

414
00:27:55,100 --> 00:27:58,170
Oh, just see what you can do,
Shrimpie.

415
00:27:58,220 --> 00:28:02,530
Aunt Violet - if I can still call
you Aunt Violet -

416
00:28:02,580 --> 00:28:04,730
I ought to warn you about something.

417
00:28:04,780 --> 00:28:07,570
~ I can guess what it is.
~ You won't approve.

418
00:28:07,620 --> 00:28:08,970
No. I don't.

419
00:28:09,020 --> 00:28:13,090
You may even feel the need to take
sides. Susan is your niece.

420
00:28:13,140 --> 00:28:17,610
Well, I think you're making a
serious mistake, but you may rest
easy on that score.

421
00:28:17,660 --> 00:28:20,370
I never take sides in a broken
marriage.

422
00:28:20,420 --> 00:28:22,290
Why is that?

423
00:28:22,340 --> 00:28:25,890
Because, however much the couple may
strive to be honest,

424
00:28:25,940 --> 00:28:29,610
no-one is ever in possession of the
facts.

425
00:28:29,660 --> 00:28:31,850
Are you in a hurry to leave
tomorrow?

426
00:28:31,900 --> 00:28:34,850
Not at all. In fact, at the risk of
being a burden,

427
00:28:34,900 --> 00:28:36,890
I thought I might stay another
night.

428
00:28:36,940 --> 00:28:39,810
~ When are you off tomorrow?
~ He's staying for a couple of days.

429
00:28:39,860 --> 00:28:43,700
Is he? Well, I hope we've got enough
here to amuse you.

430
00:28:47,060 --> 00:28:49,980
~ Come into the library.
~ Excuse me.

431
00:28:50,580 --> 00:28:53,570
Why not invite Miss Bunting to
dinner tomorrow night?

432
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:57,250
Mary will be back - we'll be quite a
party. Edith, persuade him.

433
00:28:57,300 --> 00:28:59,570
I don't think Papa would think it a
very good idea.

434
00:28:59,620 --> 00:29:02,970
~ What's this? ~ I wanted Tom to invite
Miss Bunting to dinner tomorrow,

435
00:29:03,020 --> 00:29:05,170
but Edith says Robert won't allow
it.

436
00:29:05,220 --> 00:29:08,540
Nonsense. Of course you must bring
her, if you like. I insist.

437
00:29:11,580 --> 00:29:14,050
~ So, you've enjoyed Bombay for the
most part? ~ Oh, yes.

438
00:29:14,100 --> 00:29:17,930
It's a wonderful city, the gateway
to the glories of the East.

439
00:29:17,980 --> 00:29:19,980
I felt very privileged.

440
00:29:21,940 --> 00:29:24,810
I gather things have not improved
with Susan.

441
00:29:24,860 --> 00:29:26,370
No.

442
00:29:26,420 --> 00:29:29,820
The times I've blessed you and Cora
for keeping Rose out of it.

443
00:29:30,180 --> 00:29:32,450
What happens now?

444
00:29:32,500 --> 00:29:35,010
Divorce, I'm afraid.
I can't see any other way.

445
00:29:35,060 --> 00:29:37,940
But my dear chap, is it worth it?

446
00:29:38,660 --> 00:29:40,700
Why can't you just live apart?

447
00:29:41,260 --> 00:29:45,100
If you'd ever been as unhappy as I
am, you'd know why not.

448
00:29:50,820 --> 00:29:53,530
Has Molesley gone to bed?

449
00:29:53,580 --> 00:29:56,170
Yes. Why?

450
00:29:56,220 --> 00:29:59,890
Mrs Patmore needs two more chafing
dishes first thing tomorrow.

451
00:29:59,940 --> 00:30:02,530
You can tell him in the morning.

452
00:30:02,580 --> 00:30:04,930
You're not so pally now, are you?

453
00:30:04,980 --> 00:30:06,980
Why are you still up?

454
00:30:08,300 --> 00:30:10,300
I just want to get this finished.

455
00:30:12,420 --> 00:30:15,130
Your father was never ill, was he?

456
00:30:15,180 --> 00:30:16,970
I'm sorry?

457
00:30:17,020 --> 00:30:20,420
Are you the one who's ill? Is that
it? Is that why you went away?

458
00:30:22,660 --> 00:30:25,610
~ What's it to do with you?
~ I'm right, aren't I?

459
00:30:25,660 --> 00:30:27,730
You're ill, and you went away to be
treated,

460
00:30:27,780 --> 00:30:30,930
and now you're trying to carry on
with the treatment yourself.

461
00:30:30,980 --> 00:30:34,180
Well, it has nothing to do with you,
so just leave me alone.

462
00:30:36,500 --> 00:30:39,970
Robert asked why we couldn't stay
together, for the look of the thing.

463
00:30:40,020 --> 00:30:42,020
I hope you see that we can't.

464
00:30:42,660 --> 00:30:44,850
But Daddy, where are you going to
live?

465
00:30:44,900 --> 00:30:47,010
Your mother will keep Upper Brook
Street.

466
00:30:47,060 --> 00:30:50,090
I'm not sure where I'm going to go
yet, but I'll find something.

467
00:30:50,140 --> 00:30:52,570
Oh, can I live with you?

468
00:30:52,620 --> 00:30:55,970
To be honest, I think you should
stay here for a while.

469
00:30:56,020 --> 00:30:58,570
The next part could be quite
unpleasant.

470
00:30:58,620 --> 00:31:00,850
I'm learning from it, though.

471
00:31:00,900 --> 00:31:04,660
I don't care how eligible some chap
may be - I'm not going to be
bullied.

472
00:31:05,420 --> 00:31:07,780
~ Fighting talk.
~ I mean it.

473
00:31:09,020 --> 00:31:12,810
You all think that now I'm
officially 'out', it's time to find
my man.

474
00:31:12,860 --> 00:31:16,780
But I'm only going to marry if I'm
totally, absolutely in love.

475
00:31:18,540 --> 00:31:20,540
Of course.

476
00:31:21,020 --> 00:31:23,700
When I find him, will you promise to
be on my side?

477
00:31:24,540 --> 00:31:28,810
Promise you won't try to force me
into a 'suitable' marriage, like you
were forced.

478
00:31:28,860 --> 00:31:32,130
In other words, you're asking for a
blank cheque.

479
00:31:32,180 --> 00:31:33,850
Yes.

480
00:31:33,900 --> 00:31:36,130
That's just what I want.

481
00:31:36,180 --> 00:31:38,490
Oh, my dearest one.

482
00:31:38,540 --> 00:31:42,530
On the subject of marriage, I have
no right to give advice,

483
00:31:42,580 --> 00:31:44,580
let alone orders.

484
00:31:49,260 --> 00:31:52,210
'You must make haste to reach
Dieppe,

485
00:31:52,260 --> 00:31:55,690
where you are to seek out a Mr
Avebury.

486
00:31:55,740 --> 00:31:59,650
He will arrange for you to be taken
aboard a packet and conveyed to
safety,

487
00:31:59,700 --> 00:32:03,130
but do not delay, if you hold your
mother dear.'

488
00:32:03,180 --> 00:32:05,500
Poor woman. I know how she felt.

489
00:32:06,700 --> 00:32:10,930
'As for your collection, Mr Avebury
will do what he can,

490
00:32:10,980 --> 00:32:15,290
but I'd rather every canvas or
carving were tossed in the sea

491
00:32:15,340 --> 00:32:19,580
to your being one extra hour in that
unhappy country.'

492
00:32:21,340 --> 00:32:23,770
I'm glad he didn't take her advice.

493
00:32:23,820 --> 00:32:26,770
He did not.
He brought crates and crates over,

494
00:32:26,820 --> 00:32:29,820
then went back after the Reign of
Terror to buy more.

495
00:32:30,420 --> 00:32:33,290
And you have preserved them safely
in this beautiful place.

496
00:32:33,340 --> 00:32:35,330
I'm glad you think it's beautiful.

497
00:32:35,380 --> 00:32:39,330
I think everything about Downton is
beautiful, including its mistress.

498
00:32:39,380 --> 00:32:42,450
~ You mustn't say such things.
~ I have to, or I'll burst.

499
00:32:42,500 --> 00:32:45,010
What's burst?

500
00:32:45,060 --> 00:32:47,650
I was just saying that...

501
00:32:47,700 --> 00:32:52,050
being allowed to touch a painting
like this will make me burst.

502
00:32:52,100 --> 00:32:55,730
~ It's wonderful to show it to someone
so appreciative. ~ Yes.

503
00:32:55,780 --> 00:32:58,860
No-one could say you're not
appreciative, Mr Bricker.

504
00:33:07,060 --> 00:33:10,530
~ I can't be hearing this correctly.
~ I'm afraid you are.

505
00:33:10,580 --> 00:33:13,690
So, you sleep with a man because he
wants to marry you,

506
00:33:13,740 --> 00:33:16,090
but now you change your mind.
It's insane!

507
00:33:16,140 --> 00:33:17,730
I'm sorry if you think so.

508
00:33:17,780 --> 00:33:20,290
Well, I won't let it happen.
I love you and you love me.

509
00:33:20,340 --> 00:33:22,090
At least, you told me so in
Liverpool.

510
00:33:22,140 --> 00:33:24,210
I know, and I thought I meant it.

511
00:33:24,260 --> 00:33:26,610
~ Well, what's changed?
~ I can't explain, exactly.

512
00:33:26,660 --> 00:33:28,650
But something has. That's all.

513
00:33:28,700 --> 00:33:31,410
Am I a bad lover? Is that what
you're trying to tell me?

514
00:33:31,460 --> 00:33:33,730
Don't be silly. It's not that at
all.

515
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:37,210
It's just, I feel I've sort of...
woken up out of a dream.

516
00:33:37,260 --> 00:33:40,490
How flattering (!) We go to
bed together and you wake up!

517
00:33:40,540 --> 00:33:43,730
I'm just not sure we have enough in
common, to make a go of things.

518
00:33:43,780 --> 00:33:45,530
Well, I refuse to accept it.

519
00:33:45,580 --> 00:33:48,210
I refuse to believe that a woman
like you, a lady,

520
00:33:48,260 --> 00:33:51,810
could give herself to a man without
first being certain that he was the
one.

521
00:33:51,860 --> 00:33:55,130
~ So, what are you saying? ~ This is
something we've got to get through.

522
00:33:55,180 --> 00:33:58,300
And we will. We will get through
this together.

523
00:34:09,980 --> 00:34:12,620
Mr Molesley. You wanted to see me?

524
00:34:13,220 --> 00:34:15,770
Only to say

525
00:34:15,820 --> 00:34:19,410
that I'm not sure I should be called
the First Footman, after all, Mr
Carson.

526
00:34:19,460 --> 00:34:21,450
~ Are you not?
~ No.

527
00:34:21,500 --> 00:34:23,770
Well, it's not like the old days, is
it?

528
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:29,290
I mean, the duties of the First
Footman are shared between a lot of
people now, aren't they?

529
00:34:29,340 --> 00:34:32,090
~ Are they?
~ Well, they should be...

530
00:34:32,140 --> 00:34:35,770
because I've got the duties of the
Second and Third Footman to deal
with.

531
00:34:35,820 --> 00:34:40,700
So, you don't feel you need the
precedence that the title 'First
Footman' confers?

532
00:34:42,460 --> 00:34:45,580
Er... well, not really, no.

533
00:34:46,260 --> 00:34:48,260
Very well.

534
00:34:48,980 --> 00:34:50,980
I'll see what I can do.

535
00:35:00,100 --> 00:35:03,250
~ I wasn't sure you'd want to come.
~ Nonsense.

536
00:35:03,300 --> 00:35:05,250
I'm flattered to be asked.

537
00:35:05,300 --> 00:35:08,690
I didn't think I would be again.
Not after last time.

538
00:35:08,740 --> 00:35:10,740
Try to be nice to him tonight.

539
00:35:12,220 --> 00:35:15,210
I will be, if he's nice to me.

540
00:35:15,260 --> 00:35:19,450
I know you don't like him, or any of
them, much,

541
00:35:19,500 --> 00:35:22,780
but you forget one thing -
they are good to me, in their way.

542
00:35:23,940 --> 00:35:25,940
And I love them.

543
00:35:28,900 --> 00:35:31,490
I don't want you to hate them.

544
00:35:31,540 --> 00:35:34,130
Just to realise that you're more
than a retainer.

545
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:38,100
I can't bear for you to waste your
life propping up a system that's
dying.

546
00:35:39,820 --> 00:35:41,820
Well, it won't die before dinner.

547
00:35:44,860 --> 00:35:47,890
Someone's dropped this in the
passage. The London Magazine.

548
00:35:47,940 --> 00:35:51,650
~ Does it belong to any of you?
~ Mr Barrow was reading it earlier.

549
00:35:51,700 --> 00:35:55,380
~ Would you give it to him when he
comes down, Miss Baxter? ~ Of course.

550
00:36:07,380 --> 00:36:11,090
Where did you get this?
Have you been in my room?

551
00:36:11,140 --> 00:36:14,170
Of course not. You dropped it in the
passage. Mrs Hughes found it.

552
00:36:14,220 --> 00:36:17,650
Mrs Hughes found it, but Miss Baxter
read it.

553
00:36:17,700 --> 00:36:20,970
I'm sorry for what you've gone
through, if what I think is correct.

554
00:36:21,020 --> 00:36:23,020
Don't you pity me.

555
00:36:23,580 --> 00:36:25,580
Don't you dare pity me.

556
00:36:29,380 --> 00:36:32,290
Did you bring the photographs of
della Francesca's Nativity?

557
00:36:32,340 --> 00:36:36,370
I certainly did. We can compare them
to the painting tomorrow.

558
00:36:36,420 --> 00:36:39,810
It would be so lovely if it does
turn out to be a study for it.

559
00:36:39,860 --> 00:36:42,730
Yes. It would be absolutely lovely.

560
00:36:42,780 --> 00:36:46,690
Mr Bricker, one more word and I will
ask you to sit somewhere else!

561
00:36:46,740 --> 00:36:50,170
He flatters her. He keeps asking her
opinion on everything.

562
00:36:50,220 --> 00:36:52,210
Well, don't you ever ask her
opinion?

563
00:36:52,260 --> 00:36:54,260
Of course I do. Sometimes.

564
00:36:54,980 --> 00:36:57,570
~ Did you have a good time in London?
~ Quite good.

565
00:36:57,620 --> 00:36:59,890
I set myself rather a difficult
task,

566
00:36:59,940 --> 00:37:02,820
and it's always a relief when it's
done, isn't it?

567
00:37:05,140 --> 00:37:08,730
~ How are your lessons going?
~ What's this?

568
00:37:08,780 --> 00:37:11,970
Miss Bunting is giving instruction
to Mrs Patmore's undercook.

569
00:37:12,020 --> 00:37:13,770
Oh, yes. I heard about that.

570
00:37:13,820 --> 00:37:16,570
~ You sound as if you don't approve.
~ I approve.

571
00:37:16,620 --> 00:37:20,100
As long as you're not making... her
unsettled.

572
00:37:20,540 --> 00:37:22,970
You don't know her name, do you?

573
00:37:23,020 --> 00:37:25,890
~ Sarah, please.
~ Of course he does.

574
00:37:25,940 --> 00:37:27,940
Daisy.

575
00:37:28,980 --> 00:37:32,970
~ Well, he knows it now.
~ I knew it before.

576
00:37:33,020 --> 00:37:36,530
And it sounds as if you're upsetting
her and Mrs Patmore.

577
00:37:36,580 --> 00:37:38,900
I think you should drop this.

578
00:37:39,420 --> 00:37:43,050
Why don't you send for her?
Bring Daisy in and ask her yourself.

579
00:37:43,100 --> 00:37:46,770
~ I'm sure she's too busy. ~ Not too busy
to answer a summons from you.

580
00:37:46,820 --> 00:37:48,820
We don't want to embarrass her.

581
00:37:49,660 --> 00:37:51,050
Carson?

582
00:37:51,100 --> 00:37:56,050
This is the busiest time of their
day, m'lord. Maybe it would be
better -

583
00:37:56,100 --> 00:37:58,170
No. Fetch her.

584
00:37:58,220 --> 00:38:00,340
And ask Mrs Patmore to come up, as
well.

585
00:38:01,860 --> 00:38:03,860
Very good, m'lord.

586
00:38:08,140 --> 00:38:10,140
Your father may regret this.

587
00:38:16,460 --> 00:38:18,810
~ I wondered if you wanted a hand.
~ Almost done.

588
00:38:18,860 --> 00:38:21,380
You could put the cases away, if
you've a mind.

589
00:38:23,940 --> 00:38:27,020
~ Did you enjoy yourself?
~ We weren't there long enough.

590
00:38:27,900 --> 00:38:31,210
I went to deliver a letter to Lord
Gillingham in Albany.

591
00:38:31,260 --> 00:38:32,970
Oh, yes?

592
00:38:33,020 --> 00:38:35,290
Then I walked into Piccadilly.

593
00:38:35,340 --> 00:38:37,340
His Lordship's lucky to live there.

594
00:38:38,020 --> 00:38:40,860
~ Do you recall Mr Green saying that?
~ Saying what?

595
00:38:41,740 --> 00:38:44,690
Jimmy heard where he lived and
called him a lucky tyke.

596
00:38:44,740 --> 00:38:47,340
He wasn't very lucky in the end,
though, was he?

597
00:38:48,180 --> 00:38:50,180
No. He wasn't lucky in the end.

598
00:38:50,420 --> 00:38:54,060
Right. I'll take these up to the
luggage room.

599
00:39:07,900 --> 00:39:11,490
~ Is something wrong with the dinner,
m'lord? ~ Not at all.

600
00:39:11,540 --> 00:39:13,610
We apologise, Mrs Patmore,

601
00:39:13,660 --> 00:39:19,410
for interfering with your duties in
this strange and inconsiderate way.

602
00:39:19,460 --> 00:39:21,450
Mrs Patmore, Carson tells me

603
00:39:21,500 --> 00:39:25,250
you feel Daisy's lessons have
disturbed the peace of the kitchen.

604
00:39:25,300 --> 00:39:28,130
~ Did you say that?
~ I don't know what I said.

605
00:39:28,180 --> 00:39:30,490
I was upset
about that other business.

606
00:39:30,540 --> 00:39:32,850
I wondered if it were true. Daisy?

607
00:39:32,900 --> 00:39:35,210
Well, I'm sorry if I've made trouble
downstairs.

608
00:39:35,260 --> 00:39:37,050
Well, you haven't. Not really.

609
00:39:37,100 --> 00:39:39,090
But I must say this, m'lord.

610
00:39:39,140 --> 00:39:43,490
Miss Bunting here has opened my eyes
to a world of knowledge I knew
nothing of.

611
00:39:43,540 --> 00:39:45,650
Maybe I'll stay a cook all my life,

612
00:39:45,700 --> 00:39:50,410
but I have choices now, interests,
facts at my fingertips.

613
00:39:50,460 --> 00:39:54,050
And I'd never have had any of that
if she hadn't come here to teach me.

614
00:39:54,100 --> 00:39:56,100
Brava. Well said.

615
00:39:57,700 --> 00:40:00,490
~ Quite a testimonial.
~ May we go, m'lord?

616
00:40:00,540 --> 00:40:02,770
We've still got the pudding and the
savouries.

617
00:40:02,820 --> 00:40:04,820
Of course. Thank you.

618
00:40:07,660 --> 00:40:10,690
Obviously, the lessons have proved
successful.

619
00:40:10,740 --> 00:40:12,740
I'm pleased to hear it.

620
00:40:13,140 --> 00:40:14,850
Are you, Lord Grantham?

621
00:40:14,900 --> 00:40:17,690
Oh, for heaven's sake, let it go.
You've proved your point.

622
00:40:17,740 --> 00:40:20,330
Have I, though? All I've proved is
that Lord Grantham

623
00:40:20,380 --> 00:40:23,610
would like us serfs to stay in our
allotted place from cradle to grave.

624
00:40:23,660 --> 00:40:26,210
There is only one thing I
would like...

625
00:40:26,260 --> 00:40:28,410
and that I would like passionately.

626
00:40:28,460 --> 00:40:31,940
It is to see you leave this house
and never come back!

627
00:40:36,900 --> 00:40:38,900
Happy now?

628
00:40:41,900 --> 00:40:45,730
Edith, dear, are you still writing
that very interesting column?

629
00:40:45,780 --> 00:40:47,290
Yes, Granny.

630
00:40:47,340 --> 00:40:49,450
Oh, you must show me some of them.

631
00:40:49,500 --> 00:40:51,500
What is the latest one about?

632
00:40:52,660 --> 00:40:56,300
What are they all about?
The way the world is changing.

633
00:41:00,940 --> 00:41:03,460
What's happening? What's all the
commotion?

634
00:41:04,820 --> 00:41:06,940
He just left the room.

635
00:41:07,100 --> 00:41:08,730
Oh, I don't mean to interrupt.

636
00:41:08,780 --> 00:41:11,650
No, no. We were just discussing the
Battle of Little Bighorn.

637
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:13,490
The Battle of Little Minx, more
like.

638
00:41:13,540 --> 00:41:16,690
To speak to His Lordship in that
manner! In his own house.

639
00:41:16,740 --> 00:41:19,770
It's true, then? I thought Mr Barrow
might be exaggerating.

640
00:41:19,820 --> 00:41:21,490
It seems he wasn't, for once.

641
00:41:21,540 --> 00:41:25,010
Mr Branson must be horrified to have
brought her here.

642
00:41:25,060 --> 00:41:26,570
I don't know.

643
00:41:26,620 --> 00:41:30,890
I like him, but sometimes I think
we've forgotten the Mr Branson that
was down here with us,

644
00:41:30,940 --> 00:41:33,890
spouting Keir Hardie between every
mouthful.

645
00:41:33,940 --> 00:41:36,970
They've made him one of them in a
way, but it's not who he really is.

646
00:41:37,020 --> 00:41:40,050
Then it makes me wonder whether
Downton is the place for him.

647
00:41:40,100 --> 00:41:43,460
A question I'm sure he's asked
himself many times.

648
00:41:44,420 --> 00:41:47,490
~ I can't stand that woman.
~ No great surprise there.

649
00:41:47,540 --> 00:41:49,810
I suppose you think I made a fool of
myself.

650
00:41:49,860 --> 00:41:53,090
~ What does it matter? ~ I don't see
that you were much better.

651
00:41:53,140 --> 00:41:56,700
Flirting and twinkling with that
ghastly travelling salesman.

652
00:42:00,860 --> 00:42:04,140
Go to sleep, and when you wake up,

653
00:42:05,180 --> 00:42:07,940
make sure you get out of bed on the
right side.

654
00:42:23,700 --> 00:42:25,610
Good night, Tom.

655
00:42:25,660 --> 00:42:28,700
And cheer up.
You gave Granny a wonderful evening.

656
00:42:37,660 --> 00:42:39,490
Well, what a to-do!

657
00:42:39,540 --> 00:42:43,570
It seems to me your friend Miss
Bunting is out to make trouble.

658
00:42:43,620 --> 00:42:46,250
She's brought me to life, Mrs
Patmore.

659
00:42:46,300 --> 00:42:50,250
All the same, she wouldn't win a
popularity contest upstairs.

660
00:42:50,300 --> 00:42:52,690
Would you like to write a letter to
the War Office?

661
00:42:52,740 --> 00:42:55,290
~ Oh, don't be daft.
~ I mean it.

662
00:42:55,340 --> 00:42:59,010
If you think Archie's been badly
treated, you must protest.

663
00:42:59,060 --> 00:43:01,890
I wouldn't know how to write a
letter like that.

664
00:43:01,940 --> 00:43:04,770
~ I can write it for you.
~ It won't do any good.

665
00:43:04,820 --> 00:43:07,610
His Lordship says there are rules in
place. He wouldn't lie.

666
00:43:07,660 --> 00:43:11,610
No, but if you protest and then,
over the years, more and more others
protest,

667
00:43:11,660 --> 00:43:13,890
then they might change their blessed
rules.

668
00:43:13,940 --> 00:43:16,170
This is your learning, isn't it?

669
00:43:16,220 --> 00:43:18,890
That's what you're trying
to show me - you're not afraid.

670
00:43:18,940 --> 00:43:20,940
No, I'm not.

671
00:43:21,460 --> 00:43:23,410
Well, go on, then.

672
00:43:23,460 --> 00:43:25,460
We'll do it tomorrow.

673
00:43:37,180 --> 00:43:40,850
~ How can we help you this time?
~ It's an odd business.

674
00:43:40,900 --> 00:43:43,930
Since all this started, they've kept
a plain-clothes man on watch

675
00:43:43,980 --> 00:43:45,970
at Lord Gillingham's set in Albany,

676
00:43:46,020 --> 00:43:50,410
and now I'm told that Mrs Bates was
seen there, loitering outside.

677
00:43:50,460 --> 00:43:52,610
Well, I know why that would be.

678
00:43:52,660 --> 00:43:56,450
Lady Mary must have wanted to see
Lord Gillingham while she was in
London.

679
00:43:56,500 --> 00:43:59,330
No doubt, Anna was sent with a
message to arrange a meeting.

680
00:43:59,380 --> 00:44:02,890
I knew you'd have something sensible
to say. Sergeant?

681
00:44:02,940 --> 00:44:05,130
Well, it seems likely.

682
00:44:05,180 --> 00:44:08,610
But from there, Mrs Bates walked
into Piccadilly,

683
00:44:08,660 --> 00:44:11,210
to the spot where Mr Green died.

684
00:44:11,260 --> 00:44:14,170
There's no possibility she might
have a grudge against him,

685
00:44:14,220 --> 00:44:15,890
that Mr Bates might share?

686
00:44:15,940 --> 00:44:19,730
Not that I'm aware.
As I recall, she rather liked him.

687
00:44:19,780 --> 00:44:23,370
~ Mrs Hughes?
~ Yes, she liked him. So she said.

688
00:44:23,420 --> 00:44:27,130
They may want to question her, but
before they do, they want me to
check one thing.

689
00:44:27,180 --> 00:44:31,100
She was definitely here on the day
of his death? Here at Downton?

690
00:44:33,220 --> 00:44:36,170
~ I believe so.
~ Surely, you can't think that Mrs -

691
00:44:36,220 --> 00:44:39,540
I'm not paid to think, Mrs Hughes.
Just to record the facts.

692
00:44:40,980 --> 00:44:44,810
~ Hong Kong? ~ A lot of the Tsarist
sympathisers ended up there.

693
00:44:44,860 --> 00:44:48,770
~ How did they survive?
~ As servants and taxi drivers.

694
00:44:48,820 --> 00:44:53,090
Milliners and prostitutes. Anything
they could lay their hand to.

695
00:44:53,140 --> 00:44:58,010
I will not suggest to which of those
callings the Princess Kuragin was
most suited.

696
00:44:58,060 --> 00:45:01,010
If you dislike her so much, why are
you going to all this trouble?

697
00:45:01,060 --> 00:45:03,300
~ Because I owe it to her.
~ Why?

698
00:45:04,180 --> 00:45:06,490
By the way, Susan has written to
Annabel

699
00:45:06,540 --> 00:45:09,330
to say that she's furious that
you've all taken me in.

700
00:45:09,380 --> 00:45:13,770
Oh, dear Susan has been in a rage
since she was playing with her dolls.

701
00:45:13,820 --> 00:45:15,890
I am proof against her tantrums.

702
00:45:15,940 --> 00:45:18,690
I would rephrase that if you want to
stay neutral.

703
00:45:18,740 --> 00:45:21,300
I won't take sides - it's true.

704
00:45:22,100 --> 00:45:25,300
But I don't think I could ever be
described as 'neutral'.

705
00:45:29,460 --> 00:45:32,130
Have you apologised to Tom?

706
00:45:32,180 --> 00:45:36,130
I never knew anyone who could
provoke me into saying so many things
I don't believe.

707
00:45:36,180 --> 00:45:38,890
But I am sorry, Tom.
Please forgive me.

708
00:45:38,940 --> 00:45:42,010
~ She knows how to goad you.
~ With the precision of a surgeon.

709
00:45:42,060 --> 00:45:44,770
~ Why do you go on with her?
~ Since Sybil died,

710
00:45:44,820 --> 00:45:48,690
I've forgotten what it's like to be
with someone who feels about things
as I do.

711
00:45:48,740 --> 00:45:51,330
That's why. Or that's been why.

712
00:45:51,380 --> 00:45:55,380
To me, it's as if you'd joined us
and now you're backing away.

713
00:46:00,340 --> 00:46:02,730
What are we doing here?

714
00:46:02,780 --> 00:46:06,370
I want to explain why I think we
should turn down Wavell's offer.

715
00:46:06,420 --> 00:46:09,970
I know. We are only the caretakers
of Downton.

716
00:46:10,020 --> 00:46:11,970
But Papa, some things have to
change.

717
00:46:12,020 --> 00:46:15,570
True. But we mustn't destroy what
we're trying to protect.

718
00:46:15,620 --> 00:46:19,730
Wavell would wreck this lovely place
for ever, with his ugly, cheap
houses.

719
00:46:19,780 --> 00:46:22,530
~ But you can't block all development.
~ I won't.

720
00:46:22,580 --> 00:46:25,370
I intend to expand, but without
spoiling.

721
00:46:25,420 --> 00:46:27,690
I'm going to make a plan and find a
solid builder

722
00:46:27,740 --> 00:46:30,450
who can fit in to the village and not
ruin it.

723
00:46:30,500 --> 00:46:32,730
~ That may be hard to achieve.
~ It may be harder

724
00:46:32,780 --> 00:46:36,570
than cashing Wavell's cheque, but
does that mean we shouldn't try?

725
00:46:36,620 --> 00:46:38,620
No.

726
00:46:38,940 --> 00:46:41,250
We will build.

727
00:46:41,300 --> 00:46:43,090
We'll even make money for the
estate.

728
00:46:43,140 --> 00:46:46,450
But we won't destroy what people
love about this place.

729
00:46:46,500 --> 00:46:48,290
Do you think that's wrong?

730
00:46:48,340 --> 00:46:50,650
No. It's not wrong.

731
00:46:50,700 --> 00:46:52,700
That's all I'm asking.

732
00:47:00,220 --> 00:47:03,010
Have any of you heard of someone
called Atticus Aldridge?

733
00:47:03,060 --> 00:47:06,370
I should think carefully before you
say one more word.

734
00:47:06,420 --> 00:47:09,850
I'm afraid you've read somewhere
that rudeness in old age is amusing.

735
00:47:09,900 --> 00:47:13,330
~ Margie feels you want the child to
be your plaything.
~ How can she say that?

736
00:47:13,380 --> 00:47:16,730
~ And you liked Mr Green?
~ Yes, I did. Very much.

737
00:47:16,780 --> 00:47:20,660
You despise the family, but I think
you forget - my child is one of
them.

